# `std::str::str_view` — Zero-Copy Slice Window Over Native `str` (`StrSliceView`)

## 1. Architectural Intent & Rust `core::str` Comparison
In Rust (`core::str`), sub-slicing `&s[start..end]` panics at runtime if `start > end` or if indices exceed length, while storing sub-slices inside a parser state struct forces contagious `<'a>` lifetime parameters across every AST and lexer type.

In Jenny's `std::str::str_view`, `StrSliceView` encapsulates a zero-copy `(start, len, source_cap)` window over Jenny's native 16-byte `str` slice (`jn_str`) with an inline structural invariant (`self.source_cap >= 0 && self.start >= 0 && self.len >= 0 && self.start + self.len <= self.source_cap`). Every sub-window operation (`sub_slice`, `advance`, `truncate_len`, `slice_str`) is guarded by explicit `requires ... else StrError` contracts and postcondition proofs (`ensures`), guaranteeing zero out-of-bounds panics, zero heap allocations, and zero `<'a>` lifetime contagion.

```jenny
module std_str_str_view

pub enum StrError {
    InvalidBounds,
    SliceOutOfRange,
    EmptySlice,
}

pub struct StrSliceView {
    start: i64 = 0,
    len: i64 = 0,
    source_cap: i64 = 0,

    invariant self.source_cap >= 0 && self.start >= 0 && self.len >= 0 && self.start + self.len <= self.source_cap
}

impl StrSliceView {
    pub fn from_bounds(start: i64, len: i64, source_cap: i64) -> StrSliceView ! StrError
        effects []
        requires source_cap >= 0 && start >= 0 && len >= 0 else StrError.InvalidBounds
        requires start + len <= source_cap else StrError.SliceOutOfRange
        ensures result.start == start && result.len == len && result.source_cap == source_cap

    pub fn len(self) -> i64
        effects []
        ensures result == self.len && result >= 0

    pub fn is_empty(self) -> bool
        effects []
        ensures result == (self.len == 0)

    pub fn end_offset(self) -> i64
        effects []
        ensures result == self.start + self.len && result <= self.source_cap

    pub fn advance(mut self, count: i64) -> i64 ! StrError
        effects [Mutate]
        requires count >= 0 && count <= self.len else StrError.SliceOutOfRange
        ensures self.start == old(self.start) + count && self.len == old(self.len) - count && result == self.len

    pub fn truncate_len(mut self, new_len: i64) -> i64 ! StrError
        effects [Mutate]
        requires new_len >= 0 && new_len <= self.len else StrError.SliceOutOfRange
        ensures self.len == new_len && self.start == old(self.start) && result == self.len

    pub fn sub_slice(self, rel_off: i64, sub_len: i64) -> StrSliceView ! StrError
        effects []
        requires rel_off >= 0 && sub_len >= 0 else StrError.InvalidBounds
        requires rel_off + sub_len <= self.len else StrError.SliceOutOfRange
        ensures result.start == self.start + rel_off && result.len == sub_len && result.source_cap == self.source_cap

    pub fn slice_str(self, raw: str) -> str ! StrError
        effects []
        requires raw.len() == self.source_cap else StrError.SliceOutOfRange
        ensures result.len() == self.len
}

@example("EX-STD-STRVIEW-01: StrSliceView zero-copy sub-slicing over native str, advance, and truncate") {
    var view = StrSliceView.from_bounds(4, 20, 64)!
    assert(view.len() == 20)
    assert(!view.is_empty())
    assert(view.end_offset() == 24)
    val sub = view.sub_slice(2, 8)!
    assert(sub.start == 6)
    assert(sub.len == 8)
    assert(sub.end_offset() == 14)
    val rem = view.advance(5)!
    assert(rem == 15)
    assert(view.start == 9)
    val tr_len = view.truncate_len(10)!
    assert(tr_len == 10)
    assert(view.end_offset() == 19)
    val req_line = "GET /api/v2/items HTTP/1.1"
    var http_view = StrSliceView.from_bounds(0, req_line.len(), req_line.len())!
    val path_view = http_view.sub_slice(4, 13)!
    val path_s = path_view.slice_str(req_line)!
    assert(path_s.eq_str("/api/v2/items"))
}

@example("EX-STD-STRVIEW-02: StrSliceView rejects out-of-range sub-slices and negative offsets") {
    val e1 = StrSliceView.from_bounds(50, 20, 64)
    assert(e1 is Err(StrError.SliceOutOfRange))
    val e2 = StrSliceView.from_bounds(-1, 10, 64)
    assert(e2 is Err(StrError.InvalidBounds))
    var view = StrSliceView.from_bounds(0, 12, 32)!
    val e3 = view.advance(13)
    assert(e3 is Err(StrError.SliceOutOfRange))
    val e4 = view.sub_slice(8, 6)
    assert(e4 is Err(StrError.SliceOutOfRange))
    val e5 = view.truncate_len(15)
    assert(e5 is Err(StrError.SliceOutOfRange))
}
```
